        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "797f9a9c-c31e-53a1-b58e-aac14ad0a56b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Siege of Port Royal (1710)\n- Organizations: Wabanaki Confederacy\n- Peoples: Mi'kmaq, Maliseet, Passamaquoddy, Abenaki, Penobscot\n- Places: Acadia, Nova Scotia\n- Concepts: British colonial control, French colonial possession\n\nFacts:\n- In 1710, the successful British siege of Port Royal began permanent British control over the peninsular portion of Acadia.\n- The British renamed the peninsular portion of Acadia to Nova Scotia after taking control.\n- The 1710 Siege of Port Royal was the first time the British took and held a French colonial possession.\n- The Wabanaki Confederacy is a First Nations and Native American confederation.\n- The Wabanaki Confederacy includes five principal nations: the Mi'kmaq, Maliseet, Passamaquoddy, Abenaki, and Penobscot.\n- The name Wabanaki Confederacy is also rendered as Wabenaki or Wobanaki and is translated roughly as People of the First Light or People of the Dawnland.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
